+          { t: "Abstract reasoning", d: "Figure series in which you look for the rule behind the series. Language and arithmetic play no role." },
+          { t: "Numerical reasoning", d: "Calculations and questions on tables and graphs, such as percentages and ratios." },
+          { t: "Verbal reasoning", d: "Texts where you decide which conclusion does and does not follow from what is written." },
+          { t: "Working and thinking level", d: "The outcome is set against a norm group, so you see how someone scores compared with similar candidates." },
+        ] },
+        tests: { title: "Testing and practising", intro: "The instruments hrmforce uses, plus the practice area where candidates can try freely.", cta: "View", kaarten: [
+          { t: "Cognitive test", d: "Measures working and thinking level and learning ability. The standard test for selection on level." },
+          { t: "Ability Scan", d: "Aptitude test showing how quickly someone grasps problems and picks up new things." },
+          { t: "Practice test", d: "Free practice questions per part, with the correct answer and the reasoning behind it." },
+        ] },
+        stappen: { title: "How it works", items: [
+          { t: "Invitation", d: "The candidate gets a link and takes the test at a quiet moment, on a laptop or desktop." },
+          { t: "Test on the clock", d: "Each part has a time limit. The whole usually takes 25 to 60 minutes." },
+          { t: "Report", d: "You see the score per part and the level compared with the norm group." },
+        ] },
+        onderbouwing: { title: "What a score means", tekst: [
+          "A raw score says nothing on its own. Only compared with a norm group does it become clear whether someone scores high or low, and that norm group should match the role. A score that is high compared with vocational candidates can be average compared with graduates.",
+          "An aptitude test says something about learning ability, not about motivation, collaboration or experience. So place it next to a personality test and an interview.",
+        ], link: { label: "Read the scientific accountability", href: "/wetenschappelijke-verantwoording/" } },
+        faq: { title: "Frequently asked questions", items: [
+          { q: "How do you practise for an aptitude test?", a: "By doing items of the same type and then reading the explanation. Practising mainly helps against nerves and for pacing. The practice test gives questions per part with the correct answer and the reasoning." },
+          { q: "What is a high score?", a: "That depends on the norm group and the role. A score is always expressed against a comparison group, not as a school mark." },
+          { q: "How long does an aptitude test take?", a: "Usually 25 to 60 minutes in total, with its own time limit per part." },
+          { q: "May you use a calculator or scrap paper?", a: "Scrap paper and a pen are usual for the numerical parts. Whether a calculator is allowed is stated in the invitation; stick to what it says." },
+          { q: "Is an aptitude test the same as an IQ test?", a: "They measure related things with a different purpose. An IQ test gives a general intelligence figure, an aptitude test measures the working and thinking level a role asks for." },
