+      en: {
+        meta: { title: "AI and assessments: what the rules say and what to watch | Hrmforce", description: "Where AI sits in recruitment, what the AI Act asks, which dates apply and which questions to ask a supplier." },
+        crumb: "AI in assessments",
+        hero: { eyebrow: "AI", title: "AI and assessments", lead: "AI now sits inside recruitment: in sorting CVs, in chat conversations and in summarising reports. Below you read what the law watches, which dates apply and which questions to ask a supplier." },
+        intro: [
+          "An assessment is a measurement. Little has changed there. Scoring a questionnaire happens with a psychometric model and a norm group, not with a language model forming a judgement.",
+          "What does change sits around it: pre-selection, interview notes, summaries and chat interviews. That is where AI enters, and where the rules sit. The difference between the two is what a conversation with a supplier should be about.",
+        ],
+        meet: { title: "What the AI Act says", items: [
+          { t: "Recruitment counts as high risk", d: "AI systems for recruitment, selection and evaluation sit in Annex III. That calls for documentation, human oversight, logging and information to the candidate." },
+          { t: "The date has moved", d: "Those Annex III obligations moved from 2 August 2026 to 2 December 2027 through the digital omnibus. Annex I follows on 2 August 2028." },
+          { t: "What does apply already", d: "The Article 50 transparency duty started on 2 August 2026: if someone speaks with an AI system, that has to be clear. Watermarking generated material had a run-in until 2 December 2026." },
+          { t: "Banned since February 2025", d: "Emotion recognition at work and in education is not allowed. A system inferring a mood from image or voice falls under that." },
+        ] },
+        tests: { title: "Read on", intro: "The pages this subject touches within hrmforce.", cta: "View", kaarten: [
+          { t: "Privacy and trust", d: "Which data are processed, where they sit and who can reach them." },
+          { t: "Scientific accountability", d: "What the questionnaires rest on and how scores come about." },
+          { t: "For candidates", d: "What a candidate can expect and which rights come with it." },
+          { t: "Assessment integrity", d: "How to keep an online assessment honest now that candidates can use AI themselves." },
+        ] },
+        stappen: { title: "Keeping it workable", items: [
+          { t: "Write down where AI sits", d: "List every place in your process where a model reads or writes along. Without that overview you cannot substantiate the rest." },
+          { t: "Keep the decision with a person", d: "A model may sort or summarise. The decision to reject someone belongs with a person who can explain it." },
+          { t: "Tell the candidate what happens", d: "A line in the invitation about what is measured and what happens with the outcome is the minimum. Since August 2026 it is also required for AI conversations." },
+          { t: "Keep a record of what you used", d: "Which version of which system, with which input and which outcome. That is the core of what supervision will ask for." },
+        ] },
+        onderbouwing: { title: "How hrmforce handles this", tekst: [
+          "The hrmforce questionnaires are scored with psychometric models and norm groups. How heavily an outcome weighs in a decision is something you set in your own procedure.",
+          "If you want to know how a score comes about or which data are kept, that is on the scientific accountability and privacy pages. Always ask a supplier for the same: what the model was trained on, who reviews it and how long data are kept.",
+        ], link: { label: "Read how hrmforce handles data", href: "/trust/" } },
+        faq: { title: "Frequently asked questions", items: [
+          { q: "May an algorithm decide about a candidate?", a: "The GDPR gives candidates the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing that has legal effects. A rejection counts as one. So set up your process in a way where a person takes the decision and can explain it, and record which thresholds you use." },
+          { q: "Does an online assessment fall under the AI Act?", a: "A classic questionnaire with a fixed scoring key is not an AI system in the sense of the regulation. As soon as a model that learns or decides is added, for instance for pre-selection or a chat interview, you do end up in Annex III." },
+          { q: "May you use AI to summarise an interview?", a: "You may, provided you tell the candidate you do it, check the summary before using it and do not keep the recording longer than needed." },
+          { q: "Does this apply outside the EU?", a: "The regulation covers systems used in the EU, also when the supplier sits outside it. If you recruit within the EU, it applies." },
+        ] },
+        cta: { title: "Questions about AI in your selection process?", text: "An adviser walks through where AI sits and what that means for your procedure.", primary: "Book a demo", secondary: "View pricing" },
+      },
+      de: {
